WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.847
This briefing is a
companion to Book 08 of the

2
00:00:03.847 --> 00:00:08.362
Federal Modernization
Workbook: DNS, DHCP and IPAM.

3
00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:12.746
If an agency is going to buy or build a
platform for its name and address layer,

4
00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:15.251
what does that platform
actually have to do?

5
00:00:15.251 --> 00:00:16.275
Four parts.

6
00:00:16.275 --> 00:00:19.282
Why the name and address
layer is now a control plane,

7
00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:21.100
and what federal rules ask of it.

8
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.738
What a platform has to deliver, and
what to check before buying one.

9
00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:29.172
Who runs it, including how
divisions can manage their own DNS,

10
00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:32.275
DHCP and IPAM under central governance.

11
00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:33.450
And how to decide.

12
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.713
Vendors appear on these
slides only as examples.

13
00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:39.412
Nothing here recommends
or ranks a product,

14
00:00:39.412 --> 00:00:42.712
and the status facts carry
a date, because they change.

15
00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:49.040
Every claim comes from Book 08 or from
primary sources checked in October 2026.

16
00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.724
Almost every network connection
begins with a DNS query,

17
00:00:53.724 --> 00:00:59.165
and in a hybrid multi-cloud agency the
answer decides much more than an address.

18
00:00:59.165 --> 00:01:03.967
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 counts six jobs DNS now does:

19
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:08.454
publishing private endpoint addresses,
selecting SaaS front doors,

20
00:01:08.454 --> 00:01:14.201
enforcing security policy, anchoring
certificate revocation checks, and carrying the

21
00:01:14.201 --> 00:01:19.790
service identity and application surface
that Zero Trust policy is written against.

22
00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.767
NIST's 2026 DNS deployment
guide puts it plainly:

23
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:28.440
DNS has become more than a
service; it's a security control.

24
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:34.380
Yet most agencies run it across as
many as five independent platforms:

25
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.066
Active Directory DNS or
an on-premises grid,

26
00:01:38.066 --> 00:01:42.265
Route 53, Azure DNS, Google
Cloud DNS and VMware NSX.

27
00:01:42.265 --> 00:01:44.165
None shares state with the others.

28
00:01:44.165 --> 00:01:46.009
The result is split-brain DNS,

29
00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:51.078
the same name giving different answers
depending on where the query starts, and

30
00:01:51.078 --> 00:01:56.302
it fails quietly, as certificate errors
and access denials rather than DNS errors.

31
00:01:56.302 --> 00:02:00.765
And the forwarding rules that join the
platforms usually belong to no team at all.

32
00:02:00.765 --> 00:02:04.877
IPAM and DHCP are the address
half of the same problem.

33
00:02:04.877 --> 00:02:07.760
IPAM is the record of
who owns which block,

34
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.803
and DHCP lease data is how an address
becomes a device in the asset inventory.

35
00:02:12.803 --> 00:02:16.565
The book's answer is one
authoritative DDI policy plane.

36
00:02:16.565 --> 00:02:19.459
That's an architectural
requirement, not a product,

37
00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:22.700
and it's the yardstick for
everything that follows.

38
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.015
First, before any procurement:
no OMB memorandum, directive,

39
00:02:28.015 --> 00:02:32.301
executive order or NIST
publication names a DDI vendor,

40
00:02:32.301 --> 00:02:36.400
or requires a product category
called a DDI platform.

41
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.212
They require capabilities.

42
00:02:38.212 --> 00:02:45.698
SP 800-53's SC-20, SC-21 and
SC-22 ask for signed answers,

43
00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:51.587
validating resolvers and fault-tolerant,
role-separated name service.

44
00:02:51.587 --> 00:02:57.976
OMB M-08-23 still requires
DNSSEC on applicable systems.

45
00:02:57.976 --> 00:03:03.279
M-22-09 requires encrypted DNS
wherever it's technically supported,

46
00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:06.762
to agency-designated resolvers
that log the queries.

47
00:03:06.762 --> 00:03:13.418
Executive Order 14144's section on encrypted
DNS survived the June 2025 amendments:

48
00:03:13.418 --> 00:03:19.408
agencies enable it within 180 days
wherever clients and servers support it,

49
00:03:19.408 --> 00:03:23.496
and CISA is directed to
publish contract language

50
00:03:23.496 --> 00:03:26.824
for any product that
acts as a DNS resolver.

51
00:03:26.824 --> 00:03:32.600
Civilian agencies must use CISA's
Protective DNS for all egress resolution.

52
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.188
And TIC 3.0 lists the
matching DNS capabilities.

53
00:03:37.188 --> 00:03:39.187
On logging, be precise.

54
00:03:39.187 --> 00:03:48.312
M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31 in May 2026,
and it doesn't mention DNS at all.

55
00:03:48.312 --> 00:03:52.349
It sets retention: six months
searchable, twelve retrievable.

56
00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:57.016
CISA's Logging Reference
Architecture then maps the old DNS,

57
00:03:57.016 --> 00:04:03.237
DHCP and passive DNS categories to network
activity, and calls them foundational.

58
00:04:03.237 --> 00:04:08.525
That's guidance mapped to a baseline,
not a standalone DNS mandate.

59
00:04:08.525 --> 00:04:16.435
For addresses, BOD 23-01 asks for discovery
across all agency IPv4 space every seven

60
00:04:16.435 --> 00:04:24.115
days, BOD 26-04 asks for quarterly attestation
of public IP addresses and domains,

61
00:04:24.115 --> 00:04:31.337
M-21-07's IPv6 milestones have all passed,
and CM-8 wants an accurate inventory.

62
00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:36.351
NIST SP 800-81 Revision
3 ties these together.

63
00:04:36.351 --> 00:04:41.313
It's guidance, not a mandate, and two of
its three authors work for a DDI vendor,

64
00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:44.630
which is worth knowing
when a vendor cites it.

65
00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:49.005
Now seven capabilities,
each tied to a requirement.

66
00:04:49.005 --> 00:04:54.105
First, authoritative DNS across every
environment without split-brain.

67
00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:57.607
The platform has to hold one
system of record for zones,

68
00:04:57.607 --> 00:05:02.043
forwarding rules and policy, and
every cloud DNS service consumes it.

69
00:05:02.043 --> 00:05:07.511
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 calls this overlay management,

70
00:05:07.511 --> 00:05:12.723
not replacement: Route 53 still serves
Route 53 zones, but the policy plane

71
00:05:12.723 --> 00:05:18.192
holds each rule once and pushes it
everywhere, including to branch resolvers.

72
00:05:18.192 --> 00:05:21.477
Underneath, SC-22 asks
for fault-tolerant name

73
00:05:21.477 --> 00:05:25.280
service with internal and
external roles separated.

74
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.406
When you evaluate products,
ask whether each cloud

75
00:05:28.406 --> 00:05:31.680
integration writes to the
cloud or only reads from it.

76
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.380
The book gives examples.

77
00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.692
One product's cloud
discovery imports Route 53,

78
00:05:36.692 --> 00:05:40.343
Azure and Google zones but
can't export changes back.

79
00:05:40.343 --> 00:05:44.043
Another's Azure DNS sync
handles only simple-routing

80
00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.818
records and leaves NS records read-only.

81
00:05:46.818 --> 00:05:49.442
Each decides how a change
reaches the cloud.

82
00:05:49.442 --> 00:05:52.429
Second, DNSSEC with central key custody.

83
00:05:52.429 --> 00:05:57.337
The book's position is to sign external
zones and dot-gov delegations always,

84
00:05:57.337 --> 00:05:59.596
validate on every agency resolver,

85
00:05:59.596 --> 00:06:03.880
and sign internal zones only where
the authorization requires it.

86
00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:08.455
Rollover should be automated and audited
in one place, and NIST recommends

87
00:06:08.455 --> 00:06:13.030
a hardware security module for important
keys, usually the key-signing key.

88
00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:18.280
The clouds don't fill the gap: Route 53
and Azure DNS sign public zones only,

89
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:21.780
and the default Azure
resolver doesn't validate.

90
00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.218
And check the defaults.

91
00:06:23.218 --> 00:06:28.460
Windows Server's default
zone-signing key is 1,024-bit RSA,

92
00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:33.610
below NIST's floor, and NIOS
defaults to RSA with SHA-1.

93
00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.597
Third, DNS security: two layers in series.

94
00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:42.600
The agency's own resolvers
apply the agency's policy,

95
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.347
response policy zones or a DNS
firewall, with every hit logged.

96
00:06:47.347 --> 00:06:52.231
Then all egress recursion goes upstream
through CISA's Protective DNS,

97
00:06:52.231 --> 00:06:57.359
which blocks, redirects or sinkholes
queries that match threat indicators.

98
00:06:57.359 --> 00:07:00.703
So the platform has to forward
to it, preferably encrypted,

99
00:07:00.703 --> 00:07:04.673
and that's worth testing: CISA's
own guidance notes that some vendors

100
00:07:04.673 --> 00:07:08.573
don't support the protocols for
forwarding queries to its resolver.

101
00:07:08.573 --> 00:07:10.673
Order matters more than presence.

102
00:07:10.673 --> 00:07:14.595
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's Decision

103
00:07:14.595 --> 00:07:18.185
2 is that the agency resolver
is always the first hop.

104
00:07:18.185 --> 00:07:23.664
If the agency also uses a SASE provider's
DNS service, that service is a forwarding

105
00:07:23.664 --> 00:07:28.660
target of the agency resolver, never
the place the branch edge redirects to.

106
00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.759
Every component present,
in the wrong order,

107
00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:36.322
quietly removes the agency's own
policy, validation and logging.

108
00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:38.973
Encrypted DNS completes it.

109
00:07:38.973 --> 00:07:44.811
M-22-09 requires encrypted DNS wherever
technically supported, with endpoints

110
00:07:44.811 --> 00:07:50.347
explicitly configured to agency-designated
resolvers rather than ones they discover.

111
00:07:50.347 --> 00:07:54.238
So the platform must
terminate DNS over HTTPS

112
00:07:54.238 --> 00:07:57.822
or TLS on its resolvers
and use it upstream.

113
00:07:57.822 --> 00:08:00.402
And clients mustn't route around it:

114
00:08:00.402 --> 00:08:07.161
TIC 3.0 includes filtering that blocks
unauthorized DNS over HTTPS resolvers.

115
00:08:07.161 --> 00:08:08.960
One product detail to check.

116
00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.098
Endpoint DNS agents commonly listen
on the device's loopback address.

117
00:08:14.098 --> 00:08:18.319
They protect a roaming laptop, but an
SD-WAN edge can't redirect to them,

118
00:08:18.319 --> 00:08:22.390
so they don't replace a
network-addressable branch resolver.

119
00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.578
Fourth, IPAM as the
authority, with discovery.

120
00:08:26.578 --> 00:08:30.892
Left alone, each cloud's address
manager allocates independently,

121
00:08:30.892 --> 00:08:33.402
recreating split-brain for addresses.

122
00:08:33.402 --> 00:08:36.709
The book's rule is that every
block is reserved in the

123
00:08:36.709 --> 00:08:40.477
enterprise IPAM before a cloud
or NSX network is created.

124
00:08:40.477 --> 00:08:45.555
Where a cloud lets an external
IPAM hold allocation authority,

125
00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:50.915
use it; AWS VPC IPAM can, but only
in commercial Regions, not GovCloud.

126
00:08:50.915 --> 00:08:56.553
Elsewhere, mirror the reservations into the
native pools and reconcile continuously.

127
00:08:56.553 --> 00:08:59.827
Discovery is where IPAM
meets the directives.

128
00:08:59.827 --> 00:09:05.487
BOD 23-01 asks for automated
discovery across all agency IPv4 space

129
00:09:05.487 --> 00:09:10.540
every seven days, and you can't
scan space you haven't recorded.

130
00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:13.971
BOD 26-04 asks agencies to attest their

131
00:09:13.971 --> 00:09:18.152
public IP addresses and
domains every quarter.

132
00:09:18.152 --> 00:09:23.014
And M-21-07's IPv6-only
milestones mean IPv4

133
00:09:23.014 --> 00:09:27.877
and IPv6 have to be
planned in the same tool.

134
00:09:27.877 --> 00:09:31.439
Fifth, DHCP that stays
up and stays honest.

135
00:09:31.439 --> 00:09:35.805
Failover partners move and are tested
together, and both use the same

136
00:09:35.805 --> 00:09:40.477
DNS update credentials, or a takeover
leaves names that nobody can update.

137
00:09:40.477 --> 00:09:46.593
Ask how DHCPv6 stays available: in the
two products checked, Windows Server and

138
00:09:46.593 --> 00:09:52.990
NIOS, failover is documented for DHCPv4
only, which matters under the IPv6 targets.

139
00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:55.991
Set option 6, the
resolver list, centrally,

140
00:09:55.991 --> 00:10:01.640
detect rogue DHCP servers and router
advertisements, and keep lease history.

141
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.197
NIST's guide calls for it,

142
00:10:03.197 --> 00:10:07.450
because it's what turns an address
in a query log into a device.

143
00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.638
Sixth, automation.

144
00:10:09.638 --> 00:10:13.453
No rule requires an API, but
the Federal Modernization

145
00:10:13.453 --> 00:10:17.013
Workbook's Book 08's operating
model depends on one.

146
00:10:17.013 --> 00:10:21.305
Every object, addresses, DHCP,
DNS configuration and DNS data,

147
00:10:21.305 --> 00:10:26.456
should be reachable through a documented
API, so infrastructure as code,

148
00:10:26.456 --> 00:10:30.662
ITSM workflows, certificate
automation and pipelines all

149
00:10:30.662 --> 00:10:34.525
go through the same authority
and the same audit trail.

150
00:10:34.525 --> 00:10:38.056
Automation runs as
non-interactive service accounts,

151
00:10:38.056 --> 00:10:43.000
held to the same roles and scopes as people
and unable to log in to the console.

152
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.635
Expect supported Terraform and
Ansible, ITSM actions that reserve

153
00:10:47.635 --> 00:10:51.938
an address or register a record,
and a path for existing scripts.

154
00:10:51.938 --> 00:10:56.608
Check how the API authenticates;
NIOS, for example,

155
00:10:56.608 --> 00:11:02.075
added OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect
for its API in release 9.1.

156
00:11:02.075 --> 00:11:05.387
Seventh, logging to the
SIEM, sized for the load.

157
00:11:05.387 --> 00:11:08.452
The requirement here is
M-26-14's retention:

158
00:11:08.452 --> 00:11:12.657
logs searchable for six months
and retrievable for a year,

159
00:11:12.657 --> 00:11:18.875
with synchronized timestamps, available to
the agency's security operations center.

160
00:11:18.875 --> 00:11:21.407
The name layer contributes query logs,

161
00:11:21.407 --> 00:11:24.888
lease history, policy hits
and the change audit trail.

162
00:11:24.888 --> 00:11:27.300
Capacity is where designs fail.

163
00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.319
Microsoft measured about five
percent degradation from

164
00:11:31.319 --> 00:11:35.337
full Windows DNS query logging
at 100,000 queries a second.

165
00:11:35.337 --> 00:11:40.223
NIOS reporting appliances have daily
ingest limits, and after five overruns

166
00:11:40.223 --> 00:11:45.338
in a rolling thirty days the reporting
functions lock until support intervenes.

167
00:11:45.338 --> 00:11:48.165
And dnstap on NIOS drops
records rather than

168
00:11:48.165 --> 00:11:50.763
buffering them when the receiver is down.

169
00:11:50.763 --> 00:11:54.430
Size for peak, and test
with the SIEM unreachable.

170
00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.143
Before buying any DDI product,

171
00:11:57.143 --> 00:12:01.305
check the evidence an assessor will
ask for, not the feature list.

172
00:12:01.305 --> 00:12:05.830
Start with FedRAMP, and read the
listing's scope, not just its badge.

173
00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:09.268
FedRAMP certifies a specific
cloud service offering.

174
00:12:09.268 --> 00:12:13.303
A SaaS management plane needs a
certification at the right level,

175
00:12:13.303 --> 00:12:16.442
and the agency still
authorizes its own DDI system.

176
00:12:16.442 --> 00:12:21.044
Software and appliances the agency
runs aren't covered by any listing;

177
00:12:21.044 --> 00:12:23.742
they sit inside the agency's own boundary.

178
00:12:23.742 --> 00:12:28.018
And a Moderate certification
doesn't cover High or DoD workloads.

179
00:12:28.018 --> 00:12:29.918
Then the security baselines.

180
00:12:29.918 --> 00:12:35.229
Especially for DoD systems, ask whether
DISA publishes a vendor-specific STIG,

181
00:12:35.229 --> 00:12:39.968
or whether only the generic DNS
security requirements guide applies.

182
00:12:39.968 --> 00:12:45.742
Ask for an active FIPS 140-3 certificate,
and look up its status yourself.

183
00:12:45.742 --> 00:12:51.758
FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to NIST's
historical list on September 21,

184
00:12:51.758 --> 00:12:57.367
2026, and historical modules may
be used for existing systems only.

185
00:12:57.367 --> 00:13:00.083
So a new deployment that
relies on validated

186
00:13:00.083 --> 00:13:03.167
cryptography can't cite
a historical certificate.

187
00:13:03.167 --> 00:13:04.567
Ask about NIAP.

188
00:13:04.567 --> 00:13:08.453
And know that CISA's CDM
Approved Products List isn't

189
00:13:08.453 --> 00:13:12.955
accepting new submissions; the
latest list is from April 2025.

190
00:13:12.955 --> 00:13:18.505
Advisory response: does the vendor track
the open-source DNS code underneath?

191
00:13:18.505 --> 00:13:22.307
CISA added an old BIND
vulnerability to its Known

192
00:13:22.307 --> 00:13:26.380
Exploited Vulnerabilities
catalog on October 8, 2026.

193
00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:28.630
And licensing: know what's metered.

194
00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:34.620
Azure's DNS Private Resolver bills 180
dollars a month per endpoint at list price;

195
00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.805
one DDI vendor's SaaS uses subscription
tokens that expire at term end.

196
00:13:39.805 --> 00:13:42.060
Model three to five years.

197
00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:48.760
Here's what those checks returned for
four DDI products on October 22, 2026.

198
00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.136
It's a status snapshot, not a ranking,

199
00:13:51.136 --> 00:13:54.822
and any cell can change, so
confirm on the day you decide.

200
00:13:54.822 --> 00:13:55.660
FedRAMP.

201
00:13:55.660 --> 00:14:01.383
One of the four has a listing: Infoblox
Government Cloud, Certified at Class C,

202
00:14:01.383 --> 00:14:06.197
Moderate, as SaaS in AWS GovCloud,
with one agency authorization.

203
00:14:06.197 --> 00:14:10.348
It covers the SaaS, not
on-premises NIOS appliances.

204
00:14:10.348 --> 00:14:14.122
BlueCat, EfficientIP and
Micetro have no listing.

205
00:14:14.122 --> 00:14:18.966
Their products run as software or appliances
inside the agency's own boundary,

206
00:14:18.966 --> 00:14:21.798
where the agency's
authorization covers them.

207
00:14:21.798 --> 00:14:22.710
STIGs.

208
00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:28.395
DISA publishes vendor-specific
STIGs for Infoblox: NIOS 9.x,

209
00:14:28.395 --> 00:14:33.847
version 1 release 1, and the 8.x
DNS STIG, version 1 release 4.

210
00:14:33.847 --> 00:14:35.785
None was found for the other three.

211
00:14:35.785 --> 00:14:37.260
FIPS 140.

212
00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:42.924
BlueCat holds an active FIPS
140-3 certificate, number 5506,

213
00:14:42.924 --> 00:14:48.010
a Level 1 software module
that sunsets in August 2029.

214
00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.913
Infoblox's FIPS 140-2
certificates are now historical,

215
00:14:52.913 --> 00:14:57.598
and four FIPS 140-3 modules
are in NIST's review queue.

216
00:14:57.598 --> 00:15:01.023
No certificate was found
for EfficientIP or Micetro,

217
00:15:01.023 --> 00:15:05.460
and it wasn't verified whether
BlueCat's certificate covers Micetro.

218
00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:06.360
NIAP.

219
00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.835
None of the four has a
current certification.

220
00:15:08.835 --> 00:15:15.135
Infoblox's NIOS 9.0 is in evaluation,
and BlueCat's entries are archived.

221
00:15:15.135 --> 00:15:18.960
And none appears on the
April 2025 CDM list.

222
00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.930
So every candidate has a gap to close,
in the contract or in a plan of action

223
00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:29.500
and milestones, whether that's an
authorization, a STIG or a validated module.

224
00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:35.532
Now the question many agencies care about
most: can divisions run their own DNS,

225
00:15:35.532 --> 00:15:39.188
DHCP and IPAM without
breaking central governance?

226
00:15:39.188 --> 00:15:42.788
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 says yes,

227
00:15:42.788 --> 00:15:45.588
if the platform supports a specific model.

228
00:15:45.588 --> 00:15:48.074
Corporate administrators see everything.

229
00:15:48.074 --> 00:15:53.361
Each division gets a scope limited to its
own zones, address blocks and DHCP scopes,

230
00:15:53.361 --> 00:15:56.781
with predefined manager,
operator and auditor roles,

231
00:15:56.781 --> 00:15:59.813
and automation accounts
held to the same scope.

232
00:15:59.813 --> 00:16:04.951
As an example, in Infoblox NIOS
every resource is denied by default.

233
00:16:04.951 --> 00:16:09.818
A division's administrator group gets
read-write on its own address block,

234
00:16:09.818 --> 00:16:13.568
which cascades to its networks,
ranges and reservations;

235
00:16:13.568 --> 00:16:17.478
read-write on its own zones, with
read-only on the parent view;

236
00:16:17.478 --> 00:16:22.825
and DNS and DHCP permissions, including
service restarts, on its own servers only.

237
00:16:22.825 --> 00:16:27.824
One setting matters: it makes address and
pointer records need rights on both the

238
00:16:27.824 --> 00:16:31.257
zone and the address block, so
a division with access to a

239
00:16:31.257 --> 00:16:34.913
shared zone can't point a name
into another division's space.

240
00:16:34.913 --> 00:16:37.948
And only central superusers create groups,

241
00:16:37.948 --> 00:16:42.501
roles and tag definitions, so a
division can't widen its own rights.

242
00:16:42.501 --> 00:16:44.825
Administrators must prove who they are.

243
00:16:44.825 --> 00:16:50.021
Expect PIV and CAC certificate login
checked against OCSP, and SAML single

244
00:16:50.021 --> 00:16:54.887
sign-on where the identity provider's
group decides the user's division.

245
00:16:54.887 --> 00:16:59.359
In NIOS, group names must match
exactly and the first match wins,

246
00:16:59.359 --> 00:17:01.938
so order the mappings deliberately.

247
00:17:01.938 --> 00:17:04.350
Keep a local break-glass administrator.

248
00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:07.662
And check automation scoping in
the product you're evaluating.

249
00:17:07.662 --> 00:17:10.911
In Universal DDI, service users can't sign

250
00:17:10.911 --> 00:17:14.710
in to a divisional access
view with API keys.

251
00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:17.822
Three constructs keep divisions apart.

252
00:17:17.822 --> 00:17:20.198
First, separate address spaces.

253
00:17:20.198 --> 00:17:25.380
When a lab's private range overlaps
headquarters and renumbering isn't practical,

254
00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:30.259
IPAM needs a separate routing domain,
so the same addresses can exist twice.

255
00:17:30.259 --> 00:17:36.035
Infoblox calls these network views;
Universal DDI uses IP spaces.

256
00:17:36.035 --> 00:17:37.723
Two consequences follow.

257
00:17:37.723 --> 00:17:41.637
In NIOS, a DHCP server serves
only one network view,

258
00:17:41.637 --> 00:17:46.697
so a division with overlapping
space needs its own DHCP servers.

259
00:17:46.697 --> 00:17:50.070
And NIOS doesn't enforce
uniqueness across views;

260
00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:55.827
Universal DDI's IPAM Federation does,
recording which blocks are globally unique,

261
00:17:55.827 --> 00:18:00.186
which are delegated, and which
overlap deliberately behind NAT.

262
00:18:00.186 --> 00:18:03.363
Second, DNS views, which
give different answers

263
00:18:03.363 --> 00:18:06.460
to different clients by
source address or key.

264
00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.236
The first matching view wins,

265
00:18:08.236 --> 00:18:12.010
so an external catch-all placed
first hides the internal view.

266
00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.495
Cloud services have their own versions;

267
00:18:14.495 --> 00:18:18.260
an Azure private zone answers
only the networks linked to it.

268
00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:23.060
Third, tags for ownership:
division, cost center, site.

269
00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:28.735
Infoblox calls them extensible
attributes; Universal DDI calls them tags.

270
00:18:28.735 --> 00:18:32.173
They drive filtering and
reports, not permissions.

271
00:18:32.173 --> 00:18:36.436
And a required tag is enforced
only in the graphical interface.

272
00:18:36.436 --> 00:18:40.275
Objects created through the API
or a CSV import can skip it,

273
00:18:40.275 --> 00:18:44.035
so the automation has to
enforce the division tag itself.

274
00:18:44.035 --> 00:18:49.235
One trap: in NIOS, report data isn't
filtered by object permissions.

275
00:18:49.235 --> 00:18:54.451
Granting a division the reporting app
shows it agency-wide DNS and DHCP data,

276
00:18:54.451 --> 00:18:57.710
so share dashboards per division instead.

277
00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:03.410
Delegation needs change control, and the
useful pattern is an approval workflow.

278
00:19:03.410 --> 00:19:07.535
A division's group submits a change,
with a ticket number required.

279
00:19:07.535 --> 00:19:11.697
A central approver group approves,
rejects or reschedules it.

280
00:19:11.697 --> 00:19:16.354
The task then runs with the submitter's
own permissions, so approval can't grant

281
00:19:16.354 --> 00:19:18.288
rights the division doesn't have,

282
00:19:18.288 --> 00:19:21.297
and it lands in the audit
log with a task identifier.

283
00:19:21.297 --> 00:19:27.034
That's NIOS; a Universal DDI
equivalent wasn't confirmed, so ask.

284
00:19:27.034 --> 00:19:30.210
The weakness of any workflow
is what goes around it.

285
00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:35.157
In NIOS, CSV imports don't require approval
at all, and the console warns when

286
00:19:35.157 --> 00:19:40.423
a submitter group holds import permission;
remove it from every division group.

287
00:19:40.423 --> 00:19:43.448
Service restarts aren't
subject to approval either.

288
00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:48.547
Some operations are blocked for submitters
even when they hold the permission:

289
00:19:48.547 --> 00:19:52.023
signing or unsigning zones,
key-signing key rollovers,

290
00:19:52.023 --> 00:19:55.036
resizing or splitting
networks, and bulk edits.

291
00:19:55.036 --> 00:19:59.965
And a zone delegated to a division's own
servers leaves the platform entirely:

292
00:19:59.965 --> 00:20:03.047
its changes, its response
policy and its signing.

293
00:20:03.047 --> 00:20:05.760
So decide what stays
central in every pattern.

294
00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:10.473
The superuser accounts, and the
definitions of groups, roles and tags.

295
00:20:10.473 --> 00:20:13.722
The approval workflows and
the authentication policy.

296
00:20:13.722 --> 00:20:15.672
DNSSEC keys and signing.

297
00:20:15.672 --> 00:20:20.136
Security policy, meaning response
policy zones and threat feeds.

298
00:20:20.136 --> 00:20:22.022
The top-level address blocks.

299
00:20:22.022 --> 00:20:26.930
And logging, with the platform-wide
DNS and DHCP settings.

300
00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:31.680
Those pieces make four delegation
patterns, each with a cost.

301
00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:36.730
They're drawn from Infoblox's documentation
but apply to any comparable platform.

302
00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:41.892
Pattern A: one central platform with
delegated division administrator groups.

303
00:20:41.892 --> 00:20:44.218
You get one database, one audit log,

304
00:20:44.218 --> 00:20:49.617
and one DNSSEC and response-policy posture,
with everything denied by default.

305
00:20:49.617 --> 00:20:54.861
The risks are permission sprawl, first-match
ordering surprises when roles overlap,

306
00:20:54.861 --> 00:20:58.306
the import bypass, and reports
that ignore permissions.

307
00:20:58.306 --> 00:21:02.305
Pattern B: division-owned servers
inside the central platform.

308
00:21:02.305 --> 00:21:06.580
The division gets local resilience
and can restart its own services,

309
00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:08.305
confined to its own servers.

310
00:21:08.305 --> 00:21:13.016
But it can't change platform-wide settings,
and signed zones still have a central

311
00:21:13.016 --> 00:21:15.372
hidden primary, because in NIOS only the

312
00:21:15.372 --> 00:21:17.942
Grid Master can be
primary for a signed zone.

313
00:21:17.942 --> 00:21:22.280
Pattern C: a separate network
view or IP space per division.

314
00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:26.065
It's the only way to hold
overlapping private address space,

315
00:21:26.065 --> 00:21:28.692
and it draws a clean visibility boundary.

316
00:21:28.692 --> 00:21:31.545
It needs dedicated DHCP servers per view,

317
00:21:31.545 --> 00:21:34.817
and uniqueness across
views needs federation.

318
00:21:34.817 --> 00:21:40.256
Pattern D: delegate a zone to DNS
servers the division runs itself.

319
00:21:40.256 --> 00:21:43.867
That's full autonomy,
with any DNS software.

320
00:21:43.867 --> 00:21:46.146
But central loses the audit trail,

321
00:21:46.146 --> 00:21:51.275
the response policy coverage and the
signing of that zone, and keeps only the

322
00:21:51.275 --> 00:21:56.404
parent zone, the delegation records and,
if the child is signed, its DS records.

323
00:21:56.404 --> 00:22:00.550
The patterns combine, and the
worked example uses all four.

324
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:06.353
Here's an illustrative design, not a
validated configuration, for an agency

325
00:22:06.353 --> 00:22:11.325
that owns ten dot zero slash eight and
example dot gov, with four divisions.

326
00:22:11.325 --> 00:22:14.338
Headquarters is the central
network operations team.

327
00:22:14.338 --> 00:22:18.638
It holds its own slash-twelve block
and its own zone under pattern A,

328
00:22:18.638 --> 00:22:22.938
and it hosts the central superusers
and a read-only approver group.

329
00:22:22.938 --> 00:22:24.709
If example dot gov is signed,

330
00:22:24.709 --> 00:22:28.250
the central management node
signs it as hidden primary.

331
00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:32.338
The law-enforcement bureau needs
confidentiality from the other divisions.

332
00:22:32.338 --> 00:22:36.811
It gets its own block and its own
zone, served from its own two servers,

333
00:22:36.811 --> 00:22:41.525
patterns A and B, and nothing else,
because everything else stays denied.

334
00:22:41.525 --> 00:22:43.875
Its data stays out of shared dashboards.

335
00:22:43.875 --> 00:22:47.374
If its change detail shouldn't
reach headquarters approvers,

336
00:22:47.374 --> 00:22:50.187
a bureau-lead approver
group can own its workflow.

337
00:22:50.187 --> 00:22:54.938
The regional office still
runs Windows DNS and DHCP.

338
00:22:54.938 --> 00:23:00.427
The platform manages those servers, and the
region's group gets rights on them only,

339
00:23:00.427 --> 00:23:04.301
never the platform-wide DNS
and DHCP administrator roles.

340
00:23:04.301 --> 00:23:07.438
Each server's network view
is fixed at first sync.

341
00:23:07.438 --> 00:23:11.738
The research lab numbered itself in
a range that overlaps headquarters.

342
00:23:11.738 --> 00:23:16.439
It gets its own network view or
IP space and its own DHCP pair,

343
00:23:16.439 --> 00:23:21.812
pattern C, and its zone is delegated
to lab-run BIND servers, pattern D.

344
00:23:21.812 --> 00:23:25.424
The lab hands central the DS
records for its signed zone,

345
00:23:25.424 --> 00:23:30.250
and federation records its space as
an overlapping block behind NAT.

346
00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:32.300
Who decides all this?

347
00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:36.292
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08's Chapter 09

348
00:23:36.292 --> 00:23:40.450
gives one decider per row, and
separates deciding from doing.

349
00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:45.027
The sponsor and CIO decide the
platform and its deployment model,

350
00:23:45.027 --> 00:23:48.927
with the DNS/DDI team doing
the evaluation and the CISO,

351
00:23:48.927 --> 00:23:53.250
the authorizing official and
the operating teams consulted.

352
00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:58.100
They also decide how divisions are scoped,
and break ties over the resolver path.

353
00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:03.231
The authorizing official decides whether
a cloud-delivered management plane is

354
00:24:03.231 --> 00:24:07.532
authorized for the agency's use,
and accepts the standing exceptions:

355
00:24:07.532 --> 00:24:10.625
an internal zone that loses
DNSSEC in its new home,

356
00:24:10.625 --> 00:24:14.775
or an encrypted DNS exception that
has outlived the agency's limit.

357
00:24:14.775 --> 00:24:18.310
The CISO and ISSO set
the control requirements:

358
00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:23.260
DNSSEC signing of public zones and
validation on agency resolvers,

359
00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:28.475
encrypted DNS policy, and Protective
DNS forwarding and query logging.

360
00:24:28.475 --> 00:24:34.504
The DNS/DDI team decides the namespace and
the delegation of zones to divisions and

361
00:24:34.504 --> 00:24:38.873
applications, owns the address
plan and its top-level blocks,

362
00:24:38.873 --> 00:24:42.980
and approves high-impact changes:
zones, NS and DS records,

363
00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:46.562
forwarding rules, wildcards
and response policy.

364
00:24:46.562 --> 00:24:51.434
Standard record changes inside a division's
delegated namespace are pre-approved,

365
00:24:51.434 --> 00:24:54.400
so the division or application
owner does the work

366
00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.224
through a scoped account,
without a change board.

367
00:24:57.224 --> 00:25:00.351
That team holds the most
powerful roles in the design,

368
00:25:00.351 --> 00:25:04.613
so the book scopes its automation, puts
a second approver in front of zone,

369
00:25:04.613 --> 00:25:06.744
delegation and forwarding changes,

370
00:25:06.744 --> 00:25:10.800
and keeps its privileged roles
eligible rather than standing.

371
00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:14.362
So, the decisions and the next steps.

372
00:25:14.362 --> 00:25:19.576
First, the sponsor and CIO authorize
one authoritative DDI policy plane,

373
00:25:19.576 --> 00:25:25.312
and the DNS/DDI team settles the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08's five

374
00:25:25.312 --> 00:25:29.136
configuration decisions
within 90 days and reports

375
00:25:29.136 --> 00:25:32.525
the residual risk to the
authorizing official.

376
00:25:32.525 --> 00:25:33.888
That's the book's ask.

377
00:25:33.888 --> 00:25:39.809
Second, write the requirement as capabilities,
each mapped to the rule behind it:

378
00:25:39.809 --> 00:25:44.745
signing and validation, encrypted
DNS, Protective DNS forwarding,

379
00:25:44.745 --> 00:25:48.424
discovery and inventory,
IPv6, logging retention.

380
00:25:48.424 --> 00:25:52.363
No rule names a product, so the
solicitation shouldn't either.

381
00:25:52.363 --> 00:25:56.385
Third, request the same evidence
pack from every candidate:

382
00:25:56.385 --> 00:26:00.654
the FedRAMP listing and exactly
what its scope covers, the STIG,

383
00:26:00.654 --> 00:26:05.416
an active FIPS 140-3 certificate,
Common Criteria status, the advisory

384
00:26:05.416 --> 00:26:10.424
response commitment, and a three-to-five-year
cost on the same assumptions.

385
00:26:10.424 --> 00:26:12.563
Record every gap with an owner.

386
00:26:12.563 --> 00:26:15.816
Fourth, choose a delegation
pattern for each division,

387
00:26:15.816 --> 00:26:20.156
write down what stays central, and
pilot it with one division, ideally one

388
00:26:20.156 --> 00:26:24.712
with overlapping address space, because
that's where the design gets tested.

389
00:26:24.712 --> 00:26:29.012
Fifth, the CISO sizes logging
to M-26-14's retention,

390
00:26:29.012 --> 00:26:33.413
and tests it at peak query
rates and with the SIEM down.

391
00:26:33.413 --> 00:26:35.325
The next step needs no award.

392
00:26:35.325 --> 00:26:39.948
List every DNS, DHCP and IPAM
requirement your systems inherit,

393
00:26:39.948 --> 00:26:44.400
the capability each needs, and
the evidence that would prove it.

394
00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:48.900
Book 08 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

395
00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:49.730
Thank you.
